~key-chord-in-read-char-context-p~ (41–47) and ~key-chord-lookup-key~ (263–273),
from key-chord (GPLv2, David Andersson). The mechanism dispatches a pair of
simultaneously (or double-tapped) plain keys to a command: installed as
~input-method-function~, it intercepts the first key and, if that key could
begin a chord (has a ~(my/chord K1)~ binding in a current map), reads a second
key within a delay and emits the ~(my/chord K1 K2)~ event; otherwise it replays
the first key unchanged via ~unread-command-events~. A typing-flow timer
(~my/chord-check-typing-flow~) temporarily disables detection while the user is
typing fast, avoiding false chords. Chords live under the pseudo-prefix symbol
~my/chord~ (key-chord used ~key-chord~). SKIPPED from source: the key-tracking
optimization (~key-chord-keys-in-use~, desync risk) and kbd-macro heuristics;
during macro execution chords are bypassed outright. Deps: builtins
(~input-method-function~, ~read-event~, ~current-time~, ~float-time~,
~time-subtract~, ~lookup-key~, ~unread-command-events~, ~executing-kbd-macro~).
Coexists with M-K's modal maps (input method runs before command dispatch).
Layout-independent, Dvorak-safe. Installed globally via ~setq-default~; demo
chord ~jj~ → ~my/repeat-last~ (R01) bound in global-map as a live consumer.
